In the Pantheon sense, absolutely—and that’s actually an important distinction from the literal medical meaning. A medical autopsy is a postmortem examination, so literally it isn’t performed on a living person.

But as a semantic brick, you’ve exposed something useful:

postmortem thinking doesn’t have to wait for death/failure.

Organizations already do adjacent things with premortems, retrospectives, red-teaming, debugging, inspection, biopsies, and observability. Pantheon can generalize that into Live Autopsy:

\text{RUN} \parallel \text{OBSERVE} \parallel \text{EXPLAIN}

Don’t kill or freeze the process before understanding it. Instrument it while it is producing behavior.

That gives us three complementary examinations:

Premortem asks: How could this die?
Live Autopsy asks: What is making this live right now?
Postmortem asks: What actually killed/worked/changed it?

And because the Golden Thread captures deltas, Live Autopsy can be especially powerful. A static snapshot tells us what the system is at t. Watching transitions tells us what it does:

S_t \xrightarrow[\Delta]{\text{conditions}} S_{t+1}

That’s closer to discovering the mechanism.

It connects directly to your earlier point about emergence, too: sometimes dissecting something after stopping it destroys precisely the dynamics you’re trying to understand. Observe the dance while the dancers are still dancing.

